ted States", "role": "Major literary site"}, {"name": "Walden Pond, United States", "role": "Living experiment"}, {"name": "Massachusetts, United States", "role": "Cultural backdrop"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Walden", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "walden"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q863534", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walden"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Walden contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": ""Walden" significantly shaped American environmental thought and literature, influencing key figures in social movements and inspiring a legacy of ecological awareness.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-02T17:08:23Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28607898061", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Non-fiction work by henry david thoreau Created by Henry David Thoreau.", "newValue": ""Walden," published in 1854, is a seminal non-fiction work by American transcendentalist Henry David Thoreau, reflecting on simple living in natural surroundings. Set in Concord, Massachusetts, it documents Thoreau's experiment of living in a cabin near Walden Pond for two years, amidst the backdrop of the mid-19th century American Renaissance, a period of cultural flourishing that emphasized individualism and a connection to nature. Thoreau's philosophical reflections resonate with global movements advocating for environmentalism and personal introspection, making it a pivotal text in both American literature and environmental thought.

